general_notes.general_notes:
Additional important information: (1) Statistical framework details: Generalized gamma distribution (3-parameter, best fit per BIC), fractional polynomials with standard powers {-2, -1, -0.5, 0, 0.5, 1, 2, 3}, iterative maximum likelihood with convergence threshold 0.001, natural logarithm link functions for μ and σ, identity for ν, bootstrap resampling stratified by study and sex with <1% convergence failures acceptable. (2) Methodological distinction: The paper explicitly distinguishes brain charting from brain age prediction - GAMLSS quantifies centile dispersion of MRI phenotypes on age-normed and sex-stratified distributions to assess vertical deviation from normative trajectory (typicality) rather than horizontal deviation (biological vs. chronological age). (3) Comparison to harmonization methods: Supplementary Information 5 provides detailed comparison to ComBat harmonization; GAMLSS approach chosen because it estimates study-specific parameters needed for out-of-sample centile scoring, which ComBat and GEE approaches integrate out. (4) Quality control impact: Global tissue volumes showed remarkable robustness despite extensive QC analyses; authors caution that finer-grained phenotypes (surface-based measures, functional MRI) likely require more stringent QC. (5) Sample size requirements: LOSO analyses showed study exclusion did not materially affect parameters; out-of-sample estimation requires N≥100 for stable centile scores; longitudinal analyses required N≥50 longitudinal subjects per clinical group; bootstrap used 500 replicates with <5% failure rate threshold. (6) Ethics and data sharing: Study reviewed by Cambridge Psychology Research Ethics Committee (PRE.2020.104) and Children's Hospital of Philadelphia IRB (IRB 20-017874), deemed not to require oversight as secondary analysis of de-identified data; informed consent obtained in primary studies. (7) Clinical utility caveats: Paper explicitly states the analogy to paediatric growth charts is not meant to imply immediate suitability for benchmarking or quantitative diagnosis of individual patients in clinical practice; considerable further research required to validate clinical diagnostic utility. (8) Variance modeling: Explicitly modeled age-related changes in between-subject variability with distinct patterns: GMV variability peaked at 4 years, sGMV in late adolescence, WMV in 4th decade, CSF at end of lifespan - noted as potentially important for understanding vulnerability to mental health conditions. (9) Regional heterochronicity: Spatial pattern of regional volume peaks (sensory-to-association gradient) recapitulated known gradients in brain structure and function, providing biological validation. (10) Longitudinal design note: While models are cross-sectional, validity for longitudinal application demonstrated through stability analyses; within-subject IQR < 0.05 indicates individuals 'track' their centile position over time, analogous to traditional growth charts. (11) Full list of 100+ datasets is provided in Supplementary Information 19 with acknowledgments section listing major contributors including UK Biobank, ABCD, HCP, IMAGEN, ADNI, ABIDE, ADHD-200, Developing Human Connectome Project, CALM, Cam-CAN, FinnBrain, HABS, NSPN, POND, and many others.


